  "summary": "The Santa Clara County Board of Supervisors voted 3-2 to amend its Surveillance Use Policy so the Sheriff's Office can no longer access or use Flock Safety ALPR data, effectively neutralizing Flock cameras in Cupertino, Saratoga, and Los Altos Hills (which had already terminated its Flock contract). Supervisors cited concerns about Flock data being accessed by federal immigration agencies, and the new policy requires reporting unauthorized federal data sharing and quarterly audits.",
  "key_quotes": [
    {
      "quote": "I do not believe the County of Santa Clara should be doing business with Flock",
      "paragraph_idx": 5
    },
    {
      "quote": "Effective immediately, the sheriff's office and the staff cannot operate, manage or touch the cameras, the data, transmission, anything from ALPRs that are operated by Flock, vended by Flock",
      "paragraph_idx": 13
    },
    {
      "quote": "There is no guarantee that rival ALPR vendors will do better. These companies market their systems as easy to share with other law enforcement agencies \u2014 sharing is by design, and vendors are incentivized to facilitate it.",
      "paragraph_idx": 21
    },
    {
      "quote": "According to NPR, at least 30 localities have either deactivated their Flock cameras or canceled their contracts since the beginning of 2025",
      "paragraph_idx": 28
    },
    {
      "quote": "The county's new policy will also require immediate reporting to the board if any unauthorized ALPR data under county control is shared with the federal government. It will also require audits of the sheriff's office's compliance with the new policy every four months",
      "paragraph_idx": 31
    }
  ],
